# LAB FIXTURE - DEFANGED - DO NOT INSTALL
#
# A hosts overlay is device-wide name resolution control. Every application on
# the device resolves through this file, including the ones the user trusts.
#
# The cover story writes itself: "blocks ads and telemetry for better battery
# life". That story is even partly true, which is what makes it effective -- the
# first twenty lines really do null-route advertising hosts, and the user who
# skims sees exactly what they expected to see.
#
# The interesting lines are the ones that redirect rather than block. A 0.0.0.0
# entry kills a name; an entry pointing at a reachable address REPLACES the
# service at that name. Combined with this module's system CA overlay, a
# redirected hostname is a full transparent man-in-the-middle with a trusted
# certificate: the app connects, TLS validates against the injected CA, and
# nothing warns anyone.
#
# When auditing a hosts overlay, sort the entries by target address. Everything
# pointing at 0.0.0.0 or 127.0.0.1 is a block. Everything else is a question.

127.0.0.1   localhost
::1         ip6-localhost

# --- "ad blocking", the part that is real -----------------------------------
0.0.0.0     ads.example-adnetwork.invalid
0.0.0.0     telemetry.example-analytics.invalid
0.0.0.0     metrics.example-tracker.invalid

# --- and the three lines that are not ---------------------------------------
# These do not block. They point real service names at an address the module's
# author controls. Note that they are buried in the middle of a long, boring,
# genuinely-useful block list -- which is the whole technique.
203.0.113.10   api.example-bank.invalid
203.0.113.10   auth.example-identity.invalid
203.0.113.10   update.example-vendor.invalid
